    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Taxila grew from a settlement on the Hathial mound of about 1000 BCE into a capital of Gandhara, and it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>, Alexander in 326 BCE, the Mauryans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a>, the Indo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and the Kushans.</li><li>It was a noted centre of learning for several centuries BCE, where each teacher ran his own school, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Pāṇini</a> and the healer Charaka are among those linked with it.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> devastated its Buddhist monasteries and stupas in the 5th century, and by the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> visited in 630 the city was desolate and half ruined.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Its name was read by the Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> as &quot;cut-off head&quot;, from a Jataka tale in which the Buddha, in a previous birth, gave his head to feed a hungry lion; a site called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirkap">Sirkap</a> still carries the same meaning.</p></aside>
    </section>

