    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The first major settlement is established on the Hathial mound</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">516 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> campaigns in the region, and Taxila may by then have grown significantly</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">326 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Taxiles</a> surrenders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> without a battle</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2nd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a> build a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirkap">Sirkap</a>, across the river</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">76 CE</span><span class="rf-text">Inscriptions show Taxila under Kushan rule</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">470</span><span class="rf-text">The White <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and Alchon Huns sweep over Gandhara and devastate the city's monasteries and stupas</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">630</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> finds Taxila desolate and half ruined</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bhir-mound"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhir-mound.jpg" alt="Bhir Mound" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bhir-mound">Bhir Mound</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhir_Mound,_excavation_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marco Prins, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Taxila lies on the Pothohar Plateau of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, about 25 kilometres north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamabad">Islamabad</a>–Rawalpindi area. The region was settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> era. The earliest settled occupation in the Taxila Valley was found at Sarai Khola, where three radiocarbon dates suggest that the site was first occupied between the late 4th and early 3rd millennium BCE. Its deposits held polished stone celts, chert blades and burnished pottery. Kot Diji-style wares later appeared in greater numbers, and that phase dates from the mid-late 3rd to early 2nd millennium BCE. Ruins from the Early Harappan period around 1300 BCE have also been found, though the area was eventually abandoned after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>.</p><p>The first major settlement at Taxila, on the Hathial mound, was established around 1000 BCE, and the city was possibly founded then. By 900 BCE it was involved in regional commerce, since pottery shards show trading ties with Puṣkalāvatī. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhir-mound">Bhir Mound</a> was inhabited around 800 to 525 BCE. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, the city was founded by Bharata, the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, and named in honour of his son Taksha. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> name, Takṣaśilā, translates as "City of Cut Stone" or "Rock of Taksha", and the modern name comes from the Greek rendering noted in Ptolemy's Geography.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Taxila&#8221; cites where it covers Early settlement, Ancient centre of learning:</p><ul><li>Radha Kumud Mookerji, Ancient Indian education: Brahmanical and Buddhist (Motilal Banarsidass, 1989)</li><li>Bridget Allchin, The rise of civilization in India and Pakistan (Cambridge University Press, 1988)</li><li>John Marshall, Taxila: Structural remains – Volume 1 (University Press, 1951)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sirkap"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sirkap.jpg" alt="Sirkap" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sirkap">Sirkap</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Double-Headed_Eagle_Stupa_at_Sirkap_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dawoodmajoka, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gandhara was an ancient kingdom of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and Taxila was its capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, written between the 8th and 6th centuries BCE, preserve the name of king Nagnajit of Gandhara. Excavations show that the city may have grown significantly by the time of the Achaemenid invasion in the 6th century BCE. Greek historians who accompanied Alexander described it as wealthy, prosperous and well governed.</p><p>Under the Mauryans the city lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-trunk-road">Grand Trunk Road</a>, which joined the Mauryan capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> with Puruṣapura, Puṣkalāvatī and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> beyond. In the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> it became a great seat of Buddhist learning, and one of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscriptions has been found there. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirkap">Sirkap</a> in the 2nd century BCE, local trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> controlled by the city minted most of its autonomous coinage during lulls in Greek rule.</p><p>Under the Kushans the city became well known for its trade links, which included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, sandalwood, horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, silverware, pearls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> founded Sirsukh, the latest of the ancient settlements at Taxila, and classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature of the period shows it both as a centre of culture and as a militarised border city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Taxila was sometimes ruled as part of the Gandhara kingdom, whose capital was Puṣkalāvatī, particularly after the Achaemenid period, and at other times formed its own independent district or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>. It has been suggested that it was the capital of the Achaemenid province of Hinduš between about 500 and 330 BCE. In 326 BCE its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Taxiles</a>, called Āmbhi, surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, who kept him in place as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>. After Alexander's death in 323 BCE the partitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and Triparadisus also recognised Taxiles as ruler of his realm, and the province is believed to have passed afterwards to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>.</p><p>Within a decade of Alexander's departure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> captured the provincial capital, and the Seleucid satrapies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> were formally annexed to the Mauryan Empire after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-mauryan-war">Seleucid–Mauryan war</a>. As told in Buddhist texts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> was governor at Taxila before he became king. In the 2nd century BCE the Greco-Bactrian kingdom annexed it, and several dynasties, among them one founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antialcidas">Antialcidas</a>, probably ruled from it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maues">Maues</a> overthrew its last Greek king around 90 BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondophares">Gondophares</a>, founder of the Indo-Parthian Kingdom, conquered Taxila around 20 BCE and made it his capital. Inscriptions dating to 76 CE show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises">Kujula Kadphises</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>, had captured it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> by then. In the mid-fourth century CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> is believed to have had political influence there, and many Sasanian coins found at Taxila suggest some Sasanian control.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Taxila&#8221; cites where it covers Gandhāra kingdom, Achaemenid Empire, Hellenistic period, Mauryan Empire:</p><ul><li>John Marshall, Taxila: Structural remains – Volume 1 (University Press, 1951)</li><li>Radha Kumud Mookerji, Chandragupta Maurya and his times (Motilal Banarsidass, 1988)</li><li>Romila Thapar, Aśoka and the decline of the Mauryas (Oxford University Press, 1997)</li><li>Hermann Kulke, A History of India (Routledge, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bimbisara.jpg" alt="Bimbisara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bimbisara_with_his_royal_cortege_issuing_from_the_city_of_Rajagriha_to_visit_the_Buddha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Biswarup Ganguly, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Taxila had a long reputation as a centre of learning, including the teachings of the Vedic religion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The Jatakas call it the capital of Gandhara and a great centre of learning with world-famous teachers, and show the importance of law, medicine and military science there. Students came from Kashi, Kosala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>. A student usually entered at sixteen and studied the scriptures and the Eighteen Shilpas, which included archery, hunting and elephant lore. Sons of kings, nobles, merchants, tailors and fishermen are mentioned.</p><p>Some accounts treat Taxila as one of the earliest universities in the world, while others do not, since the teachers may not have had official membership of colleges and no purpose-built lecture halls or residential quarters seem to have existed, unlike later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a>. Each teacher formed his own institution, mostly in his own house, and no king or local leader controlled the teaching. Specialisation generally took about eight years. Fees were condemned, support came from society and wealthy merchants, poor students did household work in return for board and lodging, and gurudakshina at the end could be a turban, sandals or an umbrella.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a>, the strategist who guided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>, is perhaps the best known name linked with the city, and the Ayurvedic healer Charaka studied and later taught there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Pāṇini</a>, the grammarian, was part of its community, and Pali texts name Jīvaka, physician to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a>, among its students. The Jaina tradition also holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a> Rishabhanatha gave Takshashila to his son Bahubali.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Taxila&#8221; cites where it covers In religious literature, In Jainism, Notable students and teachers:</p><ul><li>John Marshall, A guide to Taxila (Cambridge University Press, 2013)</li><li>Buddha Prakash, Political And Social Movements in Ancient Punjab (Motilal Banarsidass, 1964)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/skandagupta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/skandagupta.jpg" alt="Skandagupta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/skandagupta">Skandagupta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skandagupta_Circa_455-480_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kidarites, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Hephthalite Empire, invaded Taxila around 450 CE. The Gupta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skandagupta">Skandagupta</a> repelled them, but the city did not recover, probably because of the strong Hunnic presence in the area, the breakdown of trade and the three-way war among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the Kidarites and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> in western Gandhara.</p><p>Around 470 CE the White Huns and Alchon Huns swept over Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, causing widespread devastation and the destruction of Taxila's Buddhist monasteries and stupas, a blow from which the city never recovered. From about 500 to 540 CE it languished under the Huns ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a>, who presided over the destruction of Buddhist sites, monasteries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples across the north-west of the subcontinent.</p><p>The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> visited in 630 CE and found Taxila desolate and half ruined, with most of its sangharamas still in ruins and only a few monks remaining. He added that the kingdom had become a dependency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, with local leaders fighting among themselves for power, and had earlier been subject to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapisa">Kapisa</a>. Later Islamic geographers such as al-Biruni named a region called Takeshar, identified with the general location of Taxila, which remained under the Odi Shahi dynasty until the Ghaznavids conquered it. According to the Jaina text Prabhavakacharita, the city also suffered a plague, followed by devastation by the Turks.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Taxila&#8221; cites where it covers Hunnic invasions and decline:</p><ul><li>John Marshall, Taxila: Structural remains – Volume 1 (University Press, 1951)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="Xuanzang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xuanzang_w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The site of Taxila was rediscovered in the mid-19th century. Pliny's distances had pointed to a place on the Haro river, but Alexander Cunningham noted that Chinese pilgrims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> in particular, gave a journey of three days from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, not two, and his explorations in 1863–64 at Shah-dheri convinced him that he had found it. Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-marshall">John Marshall</a> excavated there over twenty years from 1913.</p><p>The ruins include four major cities of different periods, at three sites: Hathial and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhir-mound">Bhir Mound</a>, which date from the late 2nd millennium BCE to the 6th century BCE and from the 6th century BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirkap">Sirkap</a>, built in the 2nd century BCE by the Greco-Bactrian kings, and Sirsukh, built by the Kushans. The area also holds Buddhist monuments, among them the Dharmarajika stupa, the Jaulian monastery and the Mohra Muradu monastery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated Taxila a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1980, citing the four settlements, which reveal the pattern of urban evolution on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> over more than five centuries. The serial site has 18 components in all.</p><p>Taxila Museum holds one of the most significant collections of Buddhist stone sculpture in Gandharan art, from the first to the seventh centuries, much of it from Marshall's excavations. The modern city of Taxila, in the Rawalpindi District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, had a population of 136,900 in the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Taxila, in the Rawalpindi District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, was possibly founded around 1000 BCE and became a capital of Gandhara. Alexander, the Mauryans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a> and the Kushans held it, and it was known for its teachers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> devastated it in the 5th century.</em></p>

